  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset \"Genome - Chromosphaera perkinsii,\" published in 2017, has garnered initial scholarly attention with one citation to date. This single citation reflects its potential role in advancing research within the fields of biology and computational biology.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dataset titled \"Genome - Chromosphaera perkinsii,\" hosted on Figshare, provides genomic data pertinent to the species Chromosphaera perkinsii. Although the primary author affiliation is not disclosed, it is linked to the Multicellgenome Lab. Access to this data is open, adhering to the green open access model, which encourages broader utilization within the scientific community.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Despite being published in 2017, the dataset has recently gained traction, leading to its first citation in 2025 within an article in **eLife** that explores G-protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolutionary biology. This early citation indicates that the dataset may serve as a valuable resource in ongoing genetic research, especially concerning evolutionary relationships among metazoans.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Additionally, the dataset has demonstrated downstream reuse, with two distinct mentions in subsequent scholarly works, illustrating its relevance and applicability in the context of evolving scientific inquiries. This includes citations from credible journals that reference it in relation to G protein-coupled receptors, signifying its integration into larger discussions on biological and genomic diversity.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The impact of this dataset remains modest at present, as indicated by its online visibility metrics, which show no recorded views or downloads. Nevertheless, its role in two identified reuse instances suggests a foundational value, highlighting the importance of such datasets in enabling further research and enhancing understanding of genomic structures and functions in biological frameworks.</p>",
